WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.560
Look at one spot in a hemoglobin gene.

2
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.160
In most people, it reads GAG.

3
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.640
In some people, it reads GTG.

4
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:21.520
That one letter can bend a soft, round red blood cell into a stiff, curved sickle

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.300
shape. How can one letter change a whole cell?

6
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.180
The answer starts with how a cell uses a gene to make a protein.

7
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.220
Making a protein from a gene is called protein synthesis.

8
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.360
It has two stages.

9
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.940
In plant and animal cells, the DNA stays in the nucleus, but proteins are built

10
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:47.380
outside it. So the cell first makes a copy.

11
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.120
Transcription is the first stage.

12
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.580
An enzyme copies one gene into a strand of RNA.

13
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.760
This copy is called messenger RNA.

14
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:05.840
The enzyme reads one DNA strand, and pairs a new RNA letter with each DNA letter.

15
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:17.460
DNA letter A pairs with U. T pairs with A. C pairs with G. G pairs with C. RNA uses

16
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:24.700
U where DNA uses T. Then the messenger RNA leaves the nucleus through pores.

17
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.140
Translation is the second stage.

18
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.680
A ribosome in the cytoplasm reads the messenger RNA three letters at a time.

19
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.080
Each group of three letters is a codon.

20
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.160
Each codon stands for one amino acid, or for stop.

21
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.580
Transfer RNA brings the matching amino acid.

22
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.760
The ribosome joins the amino acids into a chain.

23
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:58.630
The codon AUG stands for methionine, and it is the usual start.

24
00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:06.010
The codons GAG and GAA both stand for glutamic acid.

25
00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.810
UUU stands for phenylalanine.

26
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.490
Then UGA means stop.

27
00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.410
It adds no amino acid, and the chain is released.

28
00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:22.790
The chain folds into a shape, and that shape lets the protein work.

29
00:02:23.583 --> 00:02:25.383
Now back to hemoglobin.

30
00:02:26.263 --> 00:02:32.583
In the usual numbering, the sixth amino acid in its chain is glutamic acid, coded by

31
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:39.543
GAG. In the sickle allele, the coding strand of the DNA reads GTG.

32
00:02:40.283 --> 00:02:46.523
The coding strand matches the messenger RNA, with T where the RNA has U. So the

33
00:02:46.523 --> 00:02:50.343
codon is GUG, and valine takes its place.

34
00:02:51.323 --> 00:02:56.543
Hemoglobin with valine can stick into stiff rods, mostly when oxygen is low.

35
00:02:57.523 --> 00:03:03.423
One changed letter can change one amino acid, and that can change a protein's shape.
